// The shipped rules. A rule maps a call's declared metadata to a policy, in
// explicit precedence order, and the first match wins.
//
// WHY RULES AND NOT MORE POLICIES. A policy is an ordered chain -- it says
// WHERE to look. Nothing in a chain can say WHICH CALLS it is for, so before
// this file every call site had to name its own policy, which is how twenty-
// eight of thirty of them ended up naming nothing and resolving a paid vendor
// model straight off the registry. A rule is the missing half: it looks at
// what the call IS -- its level, its prompt, its tags, the role acting, what
// it touches -- and picks the chain. A call site names a level and stops.
//
// EVERY RULE HERE IS @locked, AND LOCKED MEANS THREE THINGS. It evaluates
// before every unlocked rule regardless of precedence; it is re-read from the
// embedded tree on every boot, so nothing an owner did to it at runtime
// survives a restart; and no runtime-authored rule may carry the annotation or
// take one of these names. An owner adds rules beside these and may give them
// any precedence; they cannot quietly become these.
//
// PAID-LAST IS EXPRESSED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY CARE. Every rule below names
// a policy whose chain reaches the fleet first, a signed-in app second and a
// vendor last, and the federation hop asks the cost ceiling before it is
// taken. An owner who wants a paid model for everything writes a rule of
// higher precedence naming a policy that starts at that vendor: explicit,
// recorded on every decision record, and still under the ceiling.
//
// PRECEDENCE VALUES ARE DISTINCT AND GAPPED. A tie between two rules of the
// same locked-ness is a load error, because a tie is resolved by nothing and
// the rule that wins would differ between replicas. The gaps are room for an
// owner's rules to sit between these without renumbering anything.

/// The floor. It states no conditions, so it matches every call, and it cannot
/// be removed: a call that matches no rule is impossible by construction rather
/// than by care, which is why nothing downstream has to handle that case.
@when()
@policy("localFirst")
@precedence(0)
@onUnavailable("degrade")
@locked
rule default { }

/// Background work degrades freely. Nobody is waiting on the first token, so a
/// smaller model that answers is worth more here than a park that has to be
/// noticed by a person before anything moves again.
@when(tag="background")
@policy("localFirst")
@precedence(40)
@onUnavailable("degrade")
@locked
rule backgroundLane { }

/// The one stronger continuation the background lane swaps to when the cheap
/// tier is stuck on a turn -- repeated rounds where every tool call errored,
/// which usually means the model is mishandling the tool rather than that the
/// work is hard.
///
/// It raises the LEVEL and names no model. That is the whole difference from
/// the escalation policy it replaces: "stronger" used to be a second hard-coded
/// chain, so a fleet running a 70B escalated to a cloud Sonnet. Raising the
/// level asks the same question of whatever is actually available.
@when(tag="backgroundEscalation")
@level("strong")
@policy("localFirst")
@precedence(50)
@onUnavailable("degrade")
@locked
rule backgroundEscalation { }

/// An operator's agent reply reasons. This is the shipped rule that exists to
/// be READ as an example: it shows a rule raising a call's level above what the
/// prompt declared, on a condition the prompt itself cannot see.
///
/// It degrades rather than parking, unlike reasoningParks below, and the two
/// together are the distinction worth learning. A person is waiting on this
/// reply and would rather have a smaller model's answer than a card telling
/// them to open a laptop. A call that DECLARED reasoning -- emitting a
/// construct, re-planning -- would rather park.
@when(prompt="agentReply", role="operator")
@level("reasoning")
@policy("localFirst")
@precedence(60)
@onUnavailable("degrade")
@locked
rule operatorReasoning { }

/// Reasoning parks rather than degrading, and names the strongest federated
/// model rather than the cheapest.
///
/// Both halves are the same judgement. A degraded reasoning call does not
/// return a worse answer -- it returns a CONFIDENT answer produced by a model
/// that could not do the work, and nothing downstream can tell the difference
/// between that and a good one. A parked run says so and waits. Where a vendor
/// is reached at all, the extra cost of the strongest one is small against the
/// cost of a construct that compiles and is wrong.
@when(level="reasoning")
@policy("federationStrongest")
@precedence(100)
@onUnavailable("park")
@locked
rule reasoningParks { }

/// Embeddings park. A degraded embedder is not a worse vector -- it answers in
/// a DIFFERENT VECTOR SPACE, so the vector does not belong in the index it is
/// about to be written to, and the corruption is silent: every subsequent
/// similarity read returns plausible neighbours that are simply wrong.
///
/// This is the one rule whose park is not really a policy choice. The level
/// itself has no next level down, so degrading it is unrepresentable; the rule
/// states it anyway, because a reader looking for "what happens when the
/// embedder is unreachable" should find the answer here rather than infer it.
@when(level="embeddings")
@policy("localFirst")
@precedence(110)
@onUnavailable("park")
@locked
rule embeddingsPark { }
